Don't reorder an all-digits date operand that -D FMT is to read.
date reads an operand of digits only as MMDDhhmm[[CC]YY][.ss] and moves
the year to the front for parse_datestr(). It did so even with -D, whose
format reads the operand instead, so the operand reached strptime()
scrambled: date -D %Y%m%d%H%M 202610191234 complained about the invalid
date '123420261019', and digit counts other than 8, 10, and 12 were
rejected before the format saw them.

Skip the MMDDhhmm handling when -D is given.

---
 coreutils/date.c |    3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/date.c
+++ b/coreutils/date.c
@@ -210,6 +210,9 @@
 		if (date_str) {
 #if ENABLE_FEATURE_DATE_COMPAT
 			int len = strspn(date_str, "0123456789");
+			if (opt & OPT_STR2DT) {
+				/* -D FMT says how to read it */
+			} else
 			if (date_str[len] == '\0'
 			 || (date_str[len] == '.'
 			    && isdigit(date_str[len+1])
